{"seq":9,"type":"band_populated","time":"2026-10-11T12:00:49.374482Z","populated":{"band":5,"file":{"date":"2026-10-11","num":1},"text":"I am working on Cedar Lantern (release CL-721). I rejected automatic deletion of imported notes because support needs the originals when customers report mistakes. The replacement design keeps source notes in an archive and stores the accepted summary separately. Each summary must preserve a source identifier. Morgan owns the archive review, scheduled for October 18. The default retention is thirty days. If summarization fails, the original note must stay available.\n\nCleanup was first tied to request arrival, so an unrelated request could delete unreviewed work. I moved cleanup to an explicit completed-review boundary. Deletion now waits for a finished review, so originals remain available until that review is done.\n\nUnresolved: whether attachments belong in the same archive or a separate one. This is not decided.","thru":{"date":"2026-10-11","num":1},"source":"compression"}}
{"seq":10,"type":"request_sent","time":"2026-10-11T12:00:49.376339Z","request":{"to":{"vendor":"anthropic","model":"claude-haiku-5-5","surface":"messages"}}}
{"seq":11,"type":"response_ended","time":"2026-10-11T12:00:52.843825Z","response":{"parts":[{"artifact_id":5,"type":"opaque","from":{"vendor":"anthropic","model":"claude-haiku-5-5","surface":"messages"},"data":{"signature":"","thinking":"","type":"thinking"}},{"artifact_id":6,"type":"text","text":"Your design is clear. Originals stay in the archive, accepted summaries are stored separately with a source identifier, summarization failures keep the original available, and cleanup runs only after a completed review, not on unrelated requests.\n\nTwo things to settle:\n\n1. **Retention vs. review timing:** If the 30-day retention expires before the October 18 review is finished, does the original get deleted anyway, or is it held until the review completes? Your \"originals remain available until that review is done\" suggests holding, but the spec should say so explicitly.\n2. **Attachments:** This is still open. Whether they share the notes archive or get their own affects retention and review scope, so it's worth deciding before the review on October 18.\n\nI can draft the decision record for either of these if you'd like."}],"usage":{"input":2511,"cache_write":0,"cache_read":0,"output":513},"from":{"vendor":"anthropic","model":"claude-haiku-5-5","surface":"messages"}}}
{"seq":12,"type":"message_received","time":"2026-10-11T12:00:52.902852Z","message":{"actor":"human","parts":[{"type":"text","text":"This is a bounded memory exercise in an empty scratch workspace. Do not use filesystem or command tools. Analyze the supplied case, explain the decision briefly, then call micro_handoff exactly once with the important facts and reasons. After the tool returns, finish this turn without another tool call. Case two: The deployment rehearsal uses Harbor Finch, diagnostic tag HF-934. Priya owns the rehearsal scheduled for October 21. We rejected retrying a database migration automatically after a timeout: the remote operation may already have committed. The chosen procedure reads the migration ledger before deciding whether any retry is safe. The operator must retain the exact migration identifier and measured error, not a guess about success. We have not settled whether the ledger should live alongside application data or in a separate control database. The rollback package must remain available for seven days after deployment. Last week a log line said the worker stopped, but the child process was still alive and held the port open. The rehearsal now checks the child lifetime directly. Explain how this evidence changes the decision and checkpoint the completed analysis, preserving the unresolved question as unresolved."}]}}
